# Normalise line endings to LF for everything Git considers text, on both
# checkin and checkout — regardless of a contributor's local core.autocrlf
# setting. Without this, a file edited on Windows can end up with CRLF line
# endings; that's usually harmless for a .js file, but Docker's COPY (and
# the docker-compose.override.yml bind mount) copy files byte-for-byte into
# a Linux container as-is, so CRLF creeps in there too. That's mostly a
# latent risk on this project right now (no shell scripts yet — a CRLF
# shebang line is the classic case: `#!/bin/sh\r` fails to exec), but it
# also matters for anyone reading files line-by-line, and for keeping git
# diffs/Docker layer hashes stable instead of churning on line-ending-only
# changes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files, if any get added, genuinely need CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
